# =============================================================================
# .gitattributes — LINE ENDINGS, SETTLED IN THE REPOSITORY
# =============================================================================
#
# Windows ends lines with CRLF, everything else with LF. Git for Windows tries
# to paper over this with core.autocrlf=true, which converts on the way in and
# out — but that is a setting on each developer's machine, so two people
# checking out the same repository can end up with different files.
#
# This file settles it in the repository instead, where it belongs. It applies
# to everyone who clones, whatever their local configuration says.
#
# The warning it stops:
#     "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it"
#
# =============================================================================

# Detect text files automatically, store them with LF in the repository, and
# check them out with LF too.
#
# `eol=lf` on Windows sounds wrong and is not: every tool this project uses —
# Node, Vite, VS Code, browsers — handles LF perfectly. The only things that
# ever needed CRLF were old Windows programs like Notepad, and even that has
# handled LF since 2018.
#
# The alternative, `text=auto` alone, leaves the working copy up to each
# machine's autocrlf. That is how a file ends up with mixed endings, and how a
# one-word change shows up as a diff touching every line in the file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must keep CRLF, because Windows itself requires it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary files. Marking them stops Git trying to normalise their line endings,
# which would corrupt them, and stops it printing them as diffs.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary

# Generated. Collapsed in pull request diffs on GitHub, since nobody reviews a
# lock file by hand.
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true diff=none
